---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';

const title = 'How daylight saving changes international meetings';
const description =
  'A practical guide to why recurring international meetings move, how to find the risky dates, and how to keep attendees informed.';
---

<Base title={title} description={description} path="/guides/daylight-saving-international-meetings">
  <article class="max-w-2xl">
    <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" class="mb-5 text-xs text-paper-500">
      <a href="/guides" class="ring-focus hover:text-paper-900">Guides</a>
      <span aria-hidden="true" class="mx-1.5">/</span>
      <span aria-current="page">Daylight saving and meetings</span>
    </nav>
    <header class="mb-8">
      <p class="text-[11px] font-semibold tracking-[0.15em] text-paper-500 uppercase">
        Recurring meetings
      </p>
      <h1 class="mt-2 text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-balance sm:text-4xl">
        How daylight saving changes international meetings
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-paper-700">
        A weekly call can be perfectly timed for months and then suddenly arrive an hour early for
        someone. The invitation did not break; the relationship between the two local clocks did.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="mt-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">The offset is a moving relationship</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-paper-700">
        Daylight saving is set locally, not globally. Two places can both observe it yet move their
        clocks on different weekends. One place can observe it while the other never does. Cities in
        opposite hemispheres often move in opposite seasons. In each case, the difference between
        their clocks can change even though neither team changed the calendar invite.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-paper-700">
        The practical consequence is simple: a meeting anchored at 09:00 in one city is not always
        anchored at the same local hour elsewhere. Treat the local time in the meeting
        organiser&apos;s zone as the source of truth, then check every attendee&apos;s local time
        around transitions.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">A five-minute pre-flight check</h2>
      <ol class="mt-3 list-decimal space-y-2 pl-5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-paper-700">
        <li>Choose the city whose local time defines the recurring meeting.</li>
        <li>
          Add every attendee&apos;s city to the <a
            href="/overlap"
            class="ring-focus rounded font-medium text-paper-900 underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-paper-700"
            >meeting planner</a
          >.
        </li>
        <li>Check the proposed hour now, then inspect the next six to twelve months.</li>
        <li>Note every date on which a participant&apos;s local meeting time changes.</li>
        <li>Put those dates in the calendar description or send a short reminder beforehand.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">
        Choose a policy, not a one-off compromise
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-paper-700">
        For a two-person call, the least painful answer is often obvious. For a recurring team
        meeting, agree on a policy instead: keep the host city&apos;s hour fixed, rotate the burden
        every quarter, or pause when the overlap becomes unreasonable. A stated policy avoids
        treating an unpleasant time as an accident that only one person has to absorb.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Use the date, not a remembered rule</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-paper-700">
        “They change at the end of March” is not enough for planning. Rules can differ by country
        and governments can alter them. Use a converter that accepts the actual meeting date, and
        re-check important series after any calendar-system warning. Hours Apart&apos;s <a
          href="/difference"
          class="ring-focus rounded font-medium text-paper-900 underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-paper-700"
          >time-difference pages</a
        >
        show the dates on which each listed pair changes.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>
